// 🔴 DO NOT ADD `"composite": true` TO THIS PROJECT. Measured, tether#165 /
// tether#174, TypeScript 5.8.3.
//
// While no referenced project is composite, `tsc -b`'s up-to-date check looks for
// expected `.js` outputs that `noEmit: true` guarantees can never exist, so every
// run is unconditionally "out of date" and does a FULL recheck. Its own words:
//
//     Project 'tsconfig.app.json' is out of date because output file
//     'src/main.js' does not exist
//
// That is the ONLY reason the arrangement below is safe. Make this project
// composite and the up-to-date check switches to the timestamp path, the
// tsbuildinfo becomes load-bearing, and a `--noEmit` run and an emitting run then
// share one buildinfo and POISON EACH OTHER: measured on a scratch composite
// project, `tsc -b --noEmit` followed by a plain `tsc -b` exits 0 and emits
// NOTHING, reporting "is up to date because newest input is older than output
// tsconfig.lib.tsbuildinfo". Emit silently skipped, exit code 0.
//
// This is not hypothetical here, and that is why the warning is in this file
// rather than in a commit message. .github/workflows/ci.yml runs
// `pnpm typecheck` (`tsc -b --noEmit`) IMMEDIATELY BEFORE `pnpm build`
// (`tsc -b && vite build`) — exactly that pair, in exactly that order, on one
// buildinfo. Verified as it stands: typecheck leaves web/dist absent, the build
// then produces it. Add composite and that verification silently stops holding,
// with a green CI and a binary carrying whatever dist/ was lying around.
//
// The concrete hazard is a Vite scaffold: current templates DO set
// `composite: true` plus a `tsBuildInfoFile` under node_modules/.tmp/. tether#174
// deliberately did not scaffold from one for this reason. If you are regenerating
// these configs from a template, strip composite rather than keeping it.
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "useDefineForClassFields": true,
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "moduleDetection": "force",
    "noEmit": true,
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "strict": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noUncheckedSideEffectImports": true
  },

  // "test" as well as "src" — tether#174.
  //
  // Measured on this worktree at baf7117, reproducing tether#165's numbers: this
  // project's include of ["src"] type-checked 82 files, tsconfig.node.json's
  // ["vite.config.ts"] checked 1, and the five web/test/*.spec.ts were in NEITHER
  // project, i.e. 0 files. tether#165 landed a typecheck gate that can genuinely go
  // red, and its acceptance ("typecheck must be shown red") was satisfiable
  // entirely inside src/ — so the gate shipped real and the test files stayed
  // outside it, wearing the label of a fixed gate.
  //
  // That gap was about to be re-inherited: tether#173 §4 ports 11 A-grade test
  // files into the new project, three of them into web/test/. They would have
  // landed outside the type coverage again, and nothing would have said so.
  //
  // Kept in this project rather than given a third referenced project: the specs
  // import across the boundary (web/test/*.spec.ts -> ../src/lib/...) and need the
  // same lib/jsx/strictness, so a separate project would be the same options
  // written twice, with drift as its only possible future.
  //
  // 🔴 This include is NOT the gate. `include` is a claim about coverage; the gate
  // in .github/workflows/ci.yml counts `tsc -b --listFiles` output, because reading
  // a glob is how the 0 above went unnoticed for as long as it did.
  "include": ["src", "test"]
}
